window.__labLoaded({"id":"3.3","title":"The Poisson Distribution","goal":"Use the emergency room's average of 4 arrivals per hour to find the chance of exactly 2 arrivals and of a rush of 8 or more.","question":"An emergency room averages 4 patient arrivals per hour. The manager asks: how likely is exactly 2 arrivals in an hour (a quiet hour), and how likely is a rush of 8 or more? Can the Poisson distribution, which needs only the average rate, answer this?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Set the average rate","text":"The Poisson distribution only needs one number: the average count per interval, called lambda. The ER averages 4 arrivals an hour, so we build a Poisson distribution with that rate.","code":"from scipy import stats\n\nlam = 4\ner = stats.poisson(mu=lam)\n\nprint('mean     =', er.mean())\nprint('variance =', er.var())","explain":["Load the statistics tools from SciPy.","Store the average rate. The Poisson distribution is described by this one number, called lambda.","Build a Poisson distribution with that rate for the emergency room.","Print its mean.","Print its variance."],"meaning":"The mean is 4.0 and the variance is 4.0. For a Poisson distribution the mean and variance are equal, and seeing them match is the quick check that this model fits count data of this kind.","out":"mean     = 4.0\nvariance = 4.0","figs":[]},{"title":"Exactly 2 arrivals","text":"pmf gives the probability of one exact count. Notice the mean and the variance are both 4: that equality is the Poisson's fingerprint.","code":"p2 = er.pmf(2)\n\nprint(f'P(X = 2) = {p2:.4f}')","explain":["Ask for the probability of exactly 2 arrivals with pmf (probability mass function).","Print it with four decimals."],"meaning":"P(X = 2) = 0.1465, so about 14.7% of hours bring exactly 2 arrivals. That is common but not the most likely count: 3 and 4 are both likelier.","out":"P(X = 2) = 0.1465","figs":[]},{"title":"A rush of 8 or more","text":"'8 or more' is everything except 0 to 7. The survival function sf(7) is P(X > 7), which is exactly that, so it is shorter and more accurate than adding eight terms.","code":"rush = er.sf(7)\n\nprint(f'P(X >= 8) = {rush:.4f}')\nprint(f'about 1 hour in {1 / rush:.0f}')","explain":["The survival function sf(7) is the chance of more than 7, which means 8 or more.","Print the chance of a rush.","Convert it to 'about 1 hour in N' to make it easier to picture."],"meaning":"P(X >= 8) = 0.0511, about 5%, or roughly 1 hour in 20. A rush like this is unusual but not rare, so the ER should expect it a few times a week.","out":"P(X >= 8) = 0.0511\nabout 1 hour in 20","figs":[]},{"title":"See the whole distribution","text":"A table of probabilities shows where the mass sits. Most hours bring 2 to 6 patients, and the long right tail is the rare rush.","code":"import pandas as pd\n\nk = list(range(0, 11))\ntable = pd.DataFrame({'k': k, 'P(X = k)': er.pmf(k).round(4)})\n\nprint(table.to_string(index=False))","explain":["Load pandas for the table.","List the counts 0 to 10.","Build a table of counts next to their probabilities, rounded to four decimals.","Print the table without the row numbers."],"meaning":"The table peaks at 3 and 4 arrivals (0.1954 each), and most of the probability sits between 2 and 6. The tail thins out: 8 arrivals has only 0.0298, and 10 only 0.0053.","out":" k  P(X = k)\n 0    0.0183\n 1    0.0733\n 2    0.1465\n 3    0.1954\n 4    0.1954\n 5    0.1563\n 6    0.1042\n 7    0.0595\n 8    0.0298\n 9    0.0132\n10    0.0053","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"dpois(2, lambda = 4)\n1 - ppois(7, lambda = 4)\nppois(7, lambda = 4, lower.tail = FALSE)","explain":["dpois gives the chance of exactly 2 arrivals when the rate is 4.","ppois gives P(X <= 7); 1 minus it is P(X >= 8).","The same answer using lower.tail = FALSE for the upper tail directly."],"expect":"dpois = 0.1465; 1 - ppois(7, 4) = 0.0511 (the last line gives the same 0.0511)","read":"The first number is 0.1465 for exactly 2. The next two lines both give 0.0511, the chance of 8 or more, found two ways. They match Python.","note":"dpois is P(X = k), ppois is P(X <= k), and lower.tail = FALSE gives the 'more than' side directly."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (use the PDF.POISSON and CDF.POISSON functions)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / dummy.\nBEGIN DATA\n1\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE p_exactly_2 = PDF.POISSON(2, 4).\nCOMPUTE p_8_or_more = 1 - CDF.POISSON(7, 4).\nFORMATS p_exactly_2 p_8_or_more (F8.4).\nLIST p_exactly_2 p_8_or_more.","explain":["Make a tiny data set so there is one row to compute in.","Start the data block.","One row containing the number 1.","End the data block.","PDF.POISSON(k, lambda) is the chance of exactly 2 arrivals.","8 or more is 1 minus the chance of 7 or fewer from CDF.POISSON.","Show four decimals.","Print the two probabilities."],"read":"LIST shows p_exactly_2 as 0.1465 and p_8_or_more as 0.0511. These are the same as Python's answers.","note":"SPSS has no Poisson menu, so the Compute Variable dialog with PDF.POISSON(k, lambda) and CDF.POISSON(k, lambda) does the work."},"stata":{"code":"display poissonp(4, 2)\ndisplay poissontail(4, 8)\ndisplay 1 - poisson(4, 7)","explain":["poissonp(m, k) is the chance of exactly 2 when the average is 4.","poissontail(m, k) is the chance of 8 or more.","The same result as 1 minus the chance of 7 or fewer from poisson()."],"read":"The three lines print .14652511, .05113362 and .05113362. These match Python's 0.1465 and 0.0511, and the last two agree with each other.","note":"poissonp(m,k) is P(X = k) and poissontail(m,k) is P(X >= k), so 8 or more is poissontail(4, 8); poisson(m,k) is P(X <= k)."},"conclusion":"With an average of 4 arrivals an hour, exactly 2 arrivals happens 14.7% of the time and a rush of 8 or more happens 5.1% of the time, roughly 1 hour in 20. The caution: the Poisson model assumes arrivals are independent and the rate is constant, which may not hold during busy periods such as Friday nights.","practice":{"scenario":"A university help desk receives on average 6 phone calls in each 10-minute window. The supervisor wants to know how likely a very quiet window (2 calls or fewer) is, and how likely an overwhelming one (10 or more). She also wants to check the Poisson fingerprint on simulated data.","data":"Average rate lambda = 6 calls per window. For the simulation use stats.poisson(6).rvs(10_000, random_state=1).","tasks":["Print the mean and variance of the model.","Find P(X = 4), P(X <= 2) and P(X >= 10).","How often would a window be overwhelming (1 in N)?","Simulate 10,000 windows and compare the mean and variance."],"hint":"Build the distribution with stats.poisson(mu=6). Use pmf for exactly, cdf for at most and sf(9) for 10 or more.","solution":{"code":"from scipy import stats\n\ndesk = stats.poisson(mu=6)\nprint('mean, variance:', desk.mean(), desk.var())\nprint(f'P(X = 4)   = {desk.pmf(4):.4f}')\nprint(f'P(X <= 2)  = {desk.cdf(2):.4f}')\nprint(f'P(X >= 10) = {desk.sf(9):.4f}')\nprint(f'about 1 window in {1 / desk.sf(9):.0f}')\n\ncalls = stats.poisson(6).rvs(10_000, random_state=1)\nprint(f'simulated mean {calls.mean():.3f}, variance {calls.var():.3f}')","answer":"The mean and variance are both 6.0. P(X = 4) = 0.1339, P(X <= 2) = 0.0620 and P(X >= 10) = 0.0839, so an overwhelming window comes about 1 in 12. The 10,000 simulated windows had a mean of 5.978 and a variance of 5.901, both near 6, which is the Poisson fingerprint of equal mean and variance.","out":"mean, variance: 6.0 6.0\nP(X = 4)   = 0.1339\nP(X <= 2)  = 0.0620\nP(X >= 10) = 0.0839\nabout 1 window in 12\nsimulated mean 5.978, variance 5.901","figs":[]}}});
